    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#uruk">Uruk</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iraq">Iraq</a> grew into the largest city in the world in the fourth millennium BCE.</li><li>Administrators in its Eanna temple precinct produced proto-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cuneiform">cuneiform</a>, among the earliest known writing, around 3300 BCE.</li><li>It was the city of Gilgamesh and the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lugal-zage-si">Lugal-zage-si</a> before falling to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#akkad">Akkad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ur">Ur</a> and finally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#larsa">Larsa</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Warka Vase and the Mask of Warka, two of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#uruk">Uruk</a>’s greatest treasures, were looted from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iraq">Iraq</a> Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 2003 and both were later recovered.</p></aside>
    </section>

